\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Real matrix blocks control Spin module complexification}

\p{Let #{A=M_d(D)}, where #{d\geq1} and
#{D\in\{\mathbb R,\mathbb C,\mathbb H\}}, regarded as a real algebra.
Its unique irreducible real left module is #{U=D^d}, with commutant
#{D^{\mathrm{op}}} acting by right multiplication. Its complexification
has the following form:

##{\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
D&A\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb C&U\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb C
 &\dim_{\mathbb C}S\\\hline
\mathbb R&M_d(\mathbb C)&S&d\\
\mathbb C&M_d(\mathbb C)\oplus M_d(\mathbb C)&S\oplus\overline S&d\\
\mathbb H&M_{2d}(\mathbb C)&S\oplus S&2d
\end{array}}

In the complex row, the two summands have different central supports;
in the quaternionic row, they are isomorphic. For a product of real
matrix algebras, first select one simple block. These statements
apply to irreducible real Spin modules obtained from even Clifford
algebras because the actual Spin group spans the even algebra.}

\p{For the matrix-module assertion, the idempotents #{E_{ii}} split
any module into its coordinate corners, and #{E_{ij}} identify the
corners with one another. A simple module therefore has a simple
#{D}-module in its first corner, which is one-dimensional over the
division algebra #{D}; this gives #{D^d}. For an endomorphism commuting
with the full algebra, the matrix units make all coordinate maps the
same. Commutation with the #{D}-valued diagonal entries makes this
common map #{f} left #{D}-linear, so #{f(x)=xf(1)}. It is therefore
right multiplication by a single element on every coordinate.
With #{R_a(u)=ua}, composition is
#{R_aR_b=R_{ba}}, explaining the opposite algebra. Conjugation
identifies #{D^{\mathrm{op}}} with #{D} when only its real algebra
type is recorded. This is the real matrix-module classification of
\citet{Chapter I, Theorem 5.6, pp. 31--32}{lawson2016spin}.
The matrix-corner reconstruction has the general formal counterpart
\leanref{ModuleCat.matrixEquivalence}, an equivalence between
modules over a ring and modules over its matrix ring with a finite
nonempty index type. The three real types and their commutants are
the specializations calculated here.}

\p{The real row complexifies by entrywise scalar extension;
conjugation is the resulting real structure. For the other
rows, give #{U} the complex structure #{u\mapsto ui}. The
complexification of an underlying real complex space decomposes as
#{S\oplus\overline S}, the two eigenspaces of this operator after
scalar extension. For #{D=\mathbb C}, the central element #{i1}
acts as #{i} on one summand and #{-i} on the other. They are therefore
inequivalent. For #{D=\mathbb H}, right multiplication
#{J(u)=uj} commutes with the left algebra action and obeys
#{J(uz)=J(u)\overline z} and #{J^2=-1}. Thus it identifies
#{\overline S} with #{S}. The quaternion matrix embedding
\leanref/tauceti{Quaternion.toComplexMatrix_apply}
sends #{1,i,j,k} to a complex basis of #{M_2(\mathbb C)}.
Scalar extension consequently gives
#{\mathbb H\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb C\simeq M_2(\mathbb C)}
and then the displayed matrix algebra for general #{d}. Its action
on #{S} is the defining simple module of complex dimension #{2d}.}

\p{Here is the promised group-to-algebra bridge in every
nondegenerate real signature. Choose orthogonal Clifford generators
#{e_i} with #{e_i^2=\varepsilon_i\in\{1,-1\}}.
The real span #{L} of Spin is a unital subalgebra, since products
of linear combinations expand into linear combinations of group
products. For #{i\ne j} with #{\varepsilon_i\varepsilon_j=1},
#{e_ie_j} itself lies in Spin by \ref{fcap-0028}. For opposite signs put

##{v=\frac{5\varepsilon_i}{3}e_i+\frac43e_j,\qquad
Q(v)=\varepsilon_i,\qquad
g=e_iv=\frac53+\frac43e_ie_j\in\Spin(Q).}

The last inclusion again uses #{Q(e_i)Q(v)=1}, hence the actual
norm-one Lipschitz carrier. Therefore
#{e_ie_j=\frac34(g-\frac53\,1)\in L}.
All bivectors lie in #{L}; their products and #{1} span the even
Clifford algebra by its orthogonal monomial basis. Thus

##{\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\Spin(p,q)=\Cl^+(p,q).}

It follows that real invariant subspaces and commuting endomorphisms
for Spin are exactly those for the even algebra. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.span_spinGroup_eq_even_of_isSquare}
proves the spanning conclusion under a square-normalization hypothesis
for every pair of nonisotropic vectors. That hypothesis holds for a
definite real form, but fails for opposite-sign pairs. The displayed
rational combination supplies the indefinite case separately.}

\p{For the compact positive form with #{n\geq1}, the generators #{e_ie_n},
#{1\leq i<n}, square to #{-1}, anticommute, and generate the even
algebra. Equality of dimensions gives

##{\Cl^+(n,0)\simeq\Cl(0,n-1).}

Combining the real classification \ref{fcap-0016} with the three
rows above recovers the compact module types of \ref{fcap-0041}.
Lawson--Michelsohn write #{\Cl_n} for our #{\Cl(0,n)};
their even-algebra identification and module table use this convention
\citet{Chapter I, (5.4)--(5.5) and Theorem 5.8, Table III,
pp. 32--33}{lawson2016spin}.
Their compact Spin-spanning argument appears in Proposition 5.12,
p. 36; the opposite-sign calculation above extends the linear-span
argument to indefinite forms.}

\p{Signature still changes the real module type:

##{\begin{aligned}
\Cl^+(2,2)&\simeq M_2(\mathbb R)\oplus M_2(\mathbb R),\\
\Cl^+(4,0)&\simeq\mathbb H\oplus\mathbb H.
\end{aligned}}

The split case gives real two-dimensional half-spin modules.
The compact case gives quaternionic complex two-dimensional halves,
each with real dimension four.
The Lorentz even algebra #{M_2(\mathbb C)} in \ref{fcap-002L}
has a real four-dimensional simple module; its complexification
is the pair of inequivalent complex two-dimensional Weyl modules.
Thus the compact Hermitian comparison cannot simply be reused for
noncompact signatures. In all cases the scalar #{-1\in\Spin}
acts as #{-\operatorname{id}}, retaining the descent obstruction
of \ref{fcap-003M}.}
